            <ArticleTitle>Characteristics of patients with diabetes who accept referrals for care management services.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2050312115626431</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/2050312115626431</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Patients with chronic conditions can improve their health through participation in self-care programs. However, awareness of and enrollment in these programs are generally low.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We sought to identify factors influencing patients' receptiveness to a referral for programs and services supporting chronic disease management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We analyzed data from 541 high-risk diabetic patients who completed an assessment between 2010 and 2013 from a computer-based, nurse-led Navigator referral program within a large primary care clinic. We compared patients who accepted a referral to those who declined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 318 patients (75%) accepted 583 referrals, of which 52% were for self-care programs. Patients who accepted a referral had more primary care visits in the previous year, were more likely to be enrolled in another program, expressed more interest in using the phone and family or friends for support, and were more likely to report recent pain than those who declined a referral.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Understanding what factors influence patients' decisions to consider and participate in self-care programs has important implications for program design and development of strategies to connect patients to programs. This work informs outreach efforts to identify and engage patients who are likely to benefit from self-care activities.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
